{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for you a keeping.</b> Rashi's explanation: He gave them two commandments — the blood of Pesah and the blood of circumcision. As it is written in Ezekiel: ואומר לך בדמיך חיי, and again ואומר לך בדמיך חיי. And Targum Yonatan renders: בדמא דמהולתא ארחם עלך ובדמא דפסחא אפרוק יתך." "<b>And it shall be for you a keeping until the fourteenth day.</b> As for what we say in tractate Avodah Zarah — we disqualify blemishes that require inspection for thirty days — perhaps there it is to distinguish between a permanent blemish and a passing blemish, whereas here it is to know whether there is any blemish in it at all. Another interpretation: there, when one comes to acquire from the lands of the nations, thirty days are required; but here, since they had it ready in their hands, only four days were required." "<b>Until the fourteenth day.</b> So that the Egyptians would see their goddess bound in disgrace and contempt in the houses of the Jews, and hear it crying out with none to deliver it. And a proof for this matter: for here it is written לחדש הזה — to teach you that this commandment was practiced only for that Pesah." "<b>And slaughter it.</b> Whether on a weekday or on Shabbat. And how do I fulfill מחלליה מומת יומת — for other labors aside from the slaughter of the Pesah?" "<b>And slaughter it.</b> Corresponding to what Moses said: הן נזבח את תועבת מצרים לעיניהם ולא יסקלונו — HaShem said to him: ושחטו. The Pesah does not require libations, nor laying on of hands, nor waving while alive." "<b>And you shall slaughter it, all the congregation of the assembly of Israel.</b> So that no Jew can throw the matter upon his fellow and say, 'I did not do this, but another did the thing' — for all of them shall be partners." "<b>Between the evenings.</b> At the time of the gathering of the people, when laborers come back from their work."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses 